.docname {Domain}
.include {docs}

The domain is the part of the real world vCortex talks about: a building, walked through and
measured. This page describes it without any software in mind.

![The parts of a digital twin](images/domain-plan.svg "The parts of a digital twin, seen from above.")

## The survey

A **survey** is one walk through a building with a lidar.

- The robot follows a **path**: the places it passed, in order.
- Each **scan** is one turn of the laser, about 10 per second.
- All scans together make the **map**: a point cloud of everything the laser touched.

The map grows while the walk goes on. Places seen in many scans are solid; things seen in only a
few scans, like a person passing by, are not part of the building.

## The building

What a survey describes, from the largest part to the smallest:

.mermaid caption:{How the parts of a building fit together.}
    flowchart TD
        B[Building] --> L[Level]
        L --> W[Wall]
        L --> R[Room]
        L --> O[Object]
        R -. bounded by .-> W
        O -. stands in .-> R

| Part | What it is | What we know about it |
|---|---|---|
| **Building** | The whole place that was surveyed | Its levels |
| **Level** | One floor of the building | How high its floor is, and how tall its walls are |
| **Wall** | A straight, upright piece of wall | Where it starts and ends, and how thick it is |
| **Room** | The floor space that walls enclose | Its outline, the walls around it, and whether it is fully closed |
| **Object** | Anything that is not a wall or the floor: a desk, a wardrobe, a bin | Where it stands, its footprint, how it is turned, and how high it was seen |

## Rules of the domain

- **Heights start from the floor.** A floor is never perfectly level, so "1 metre high" means
  1 metre above the floor at that spot.
- **Walls are straight and upright.** A long wall with a bend is two walls.
- **A wall height belongs to the level.** All walls of a level share it. It is either measured or
  given by a person; a twin always says which.
- **Furniture is not a wall.** A wardrobe against a wall is an object in front of a wall, even when
  it hides the wall completely.
- **A room may be open.** When part of its outline was never seen, or a doorway leaves a gap, the
  room is still a room, marked as not closed.
- **An object belongs to at most one room.** An object outside every room belongs to the level.
- **What was not seen stays unknown.** An object's top is "the highest point seen", not its real
  top. The twin never invents what the laser did not see.

## Where the code differs today

The domain above is the target. The software does not cover all of it yet:

| Domain | Today |
|---|---|
| A building has many levels | One level per survey |
| Wall height can be measured | Always typed in, or the default of 2.70 m |
| Doors and windows are openings in walls | Not recorded; a gap only marks the room as open |
| An object has a kind (desk, wardrobe…) | Every object is "unknown" |
